#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Package versions\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Mga bersyon ng package\">\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The code on this page was developed using the following requirements.\n"
"We recommend using these versions or newer.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang code sa pahinang ito ay ginawa gamit ang mga sumusunod na requirements.\n"
"Inirerekomenda naming gamitin ang mga bersyong ito o mas bago.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"In quantum mechanics, observables correspond to physical properties that can be measured.\n"
"When considering a system of spins, for example, you could be interested in measuring the system's energy or obtaining information about the alignment of the spins, such as the magnetization or the correlations between spins.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Sa quantum mechanics, ang mga observable ay tumutugma sa mga pisikal na katangian na maaaring sukatin.\n"
"Kapag pinag-aaralan ang isang sistema ng mga spin, halimbawa, maaaring interesado ka sa pagsukat ng enerhiya ng sistema o sa pagkuha ng impormasyon tungkol sa pagkakahanay ng mga spin, tulad ng magnetization o ng mga kaugnayan sa pagitan ng mga spin.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To measure an $n$-qubit observable $O$ on a quantum computer, you must represent it as a sum of tensor products of Pauli operators, that is\n"
msgstr "Para sukatin ang isang $n$-qubit observable na $O$ sa isang quantum computer, kailangan mong ipahayag ito bilang kabuuan ng mga tensor product ng mga Pauli operator, ibig sabihin\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"O = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\alpha_k P_k,~~ P_k \\in \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}^{\\otimes n},~~ \\alpha_k \\in \\mathbb{R},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"O = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\alpha_k P_k,~~ P_k \\in \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}^{\\otimes n},~~ \\alpha_k \\in \\mathbb{R},\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where\n"
msgstr "kung saan\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"I = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"X = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"Y = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & -i \\\\ i & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"Z = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"I = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"X = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"Y = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & -i \\\\ i & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"~~\n"
"Z = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "and you use the fact that an observable is Hermitian, as in, $O^\\dagger = O$. If $O$ is not Hermitian it can still be decomposed as a sum of Paulis, but the coefficient $\\alpha_k$ becomes complex.\n"
msgstr "at ginagamit mo ang katotohanang ang observable ay Hermitian, ibig sabihin, $O^\\dagger = O$. Kung ang $O$ ay hindi Hermitian, maaari pa rin itong i-decompose bilang kabuuan ng mga Pauli, ngunit ang coefficient na $\\alpha_k$ ay magiging complex.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In many cases, the observable is naturally specified in this representation after mapping the system of interest to qubits.\n"
"For example, a spin-1/2 system can be mapped to an Ising Hamiltonian\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa maraming kaso, ang observable ay natural na tinutukoy sa representasyong ito matapos i-map ang sistema ng interes sa mga qubit.\n"
"Halimbawa, ang isang spin-1/2 na sistema ay maaaring i-map sa isang Ising Hamiltonian\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = \\sum_{\\langle i, j\\rangle} Z_i Z_j - \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"H = \\sum_{\\langle i, j\\rangle} Z_i Z_j - \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i,\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where the indices $\\langle i, j\\rangle$ run over interacting spins and the spins are subject to a transverse field in $X$.\n"
"The subscript index indicates which qubit the Pauli operator acts on, i.e. $X_i$ applies an $X$ operator on qubit $i$ and leaves the rest unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""
"kung saan ang mga index na $\\langle i, j\\rangle$ ay tumatakbo sa mga nakikipag-ugnayan na spin at ang mga spin ay napapailalim sa transverse field sa $X$.\n"
"Ang subscript index ay nagpapahiwatig kung saang qubit kumikilos ang Pauli operator, ibig sabihin, ang $X_i$ ay nag-aaplay ng $X$ operator sa qubit $i$ at iniiwang hindi nagbabago ang iba pang qubit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the Qiskit SDK, this Hamiltonian could be constructed with the following code.\n"
msgstr "Sa Qiskit SDK, ang Hamiltonian na ito ay maaaring itayo gamit ang sumusunod na code.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If we would like to measure the energy the observable is the Hamiltonian itself. Alternatively, we could be\n"
"interested in measuring system properties like the average magnetization by counting the number of spins\n"
"aligned in the $Z$-direction with the observable\n"
msgstr ""
"Kung gusto nating sukatin ang enerhiya, ang observable ay ang Hamiltonian mismo. Bilang alternatibo, maaaring interesado tayong\n"
"sukatin ang mga katangian ng sistema tulad ng average magnetization sa pamamagitan ng pagbibilang ng bilang ng mga spin\n"
"na nakahanay sa $Z$-direksyon gamit ang observable\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"O = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1} Z_i\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"O = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1} Z_i\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For observables that are not given in terms of Pauli operators but in a matrix form, we first have to reformulate them\n"
"in the Pauli basis in order to evaluate them on a quantum computer.\n"
"We are always able to find such a representation as the Pauli matrices form a basis for the Hermitian $2^n \\times 2^n$ matrices.\n"
"We expand the observable $O$ as\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa mga observable na hindi ibinibigay sa mga Pauli operator kundi sa matrix form, kailangan muna nating baguhin ang representasyon nito\n"
"sa Pauli basis para masuri ang mga ito sa isang quantum computer.\n"
"Palagi tayong makakahanap ng ganitong representasyon dahil ang mga Pauli matrix ay bumubuo ng basis para sa Hermitian $2^n \\times 2^n$ na mga matrix.\n"
"Pinapalawak natin ang observable na $O$ bilang\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"O =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n}\n"
"\\sum_{P \\in \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}^{\\otimes n}}\n"
"\\mathrm{Tr}(O P) P,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"O =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n}\n"
"\\sum_{P \\in \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}^{\\otimes n}}\n"
"\\mathrm{Tr}(O P) P,\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid ""
#| "where the sum runs over all possible $n$-qubit Pauli terms and $\\mathrm{Tr}(\\cdot)$ is the trace of a matrix, which acts as inner product.\n"
#| "You can implement this decomposition  from a matrix to Pauli terms using the `SparsePauliOp.from_operator` method, like so:\n"
msgid ""
"where the sum runs over all possible $n$-qubit Pauli terms and $\\mathrm{Tr}(\\cdot)$ is the trace of a matrix, which acts as an inner product. The factor $1/2^n$ accounts for the Hilbert--Schmidt normalization of $n$-qubit Pauli strings.\n"
"You can implement this decomposition  from a matrix to Pauli terms using the `SparsePauliOp.from_operator` method, like so:\n"
msgstr ""
"kung saan ang kabuuan ay tumatakbo sa lahat ng posibleng $n$-qubit Pauli term at ang $\\mathrm{Tr}(\\cdot)$ ay ang trace ng isang matrix, na gumaganap bilang inner product.\n"
"Maaari mong ipatupad ang decomposition na ito mula sa isang matrix patungong Pauli term gamit ang `SparsePauliOp.from_operator` na method, ganito:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This means the matrix can be written as Pauli terms as $O = -Z_1 + 0.5 X_2 + Y_2 Y_1$.\n"
msgstr "Ibig sabihin nito, ang matrix ay maaaring isulat bilang mga Pauli term na $O = -Z_1 + 0.5 X_2 + Y_2 Y_1$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"  Remember the tensor product order maps to qubits as $q_n \\otimes q_{n-1} \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes q_1$.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"  Tandaan na ang tensor product order ay namamap sa mga qubit bilang $q_n \\otimes q_{n-1} \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes q_1$.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"  If the observable is Hermitian (meaning $O^\\dagger = O$), the Pauli coefficients are real numbers.\n"
"  We can, however, also decompose any other complex matrix in terms of Paulis, if we allow for complex-valued coefficients.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"  Kung ang observable ay Hermitian (ibig sabihin, $O^\\dagger = O$), ang mga Pauli coefficient ay mga tunay na numero.\n"
"  Gayunpaman, maaari rin nating i-decompose ang anumang iba pang complex matrix sa mga Pauli, kung papayagan natin ang mga complex-valued na coefficient.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measure in Pauli bases {#measure-in-pauli-bases}"
msgstr "Sukatin sa mga Pauli basis {#measure-in-pauli-bases}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A measurement projects the qubit state to the computational basis $\\{|0\\rangle, |1\\rangle\\}$. This implies that you can only measure observables that are diagonal in this basis, such as Paulis consisting only of $I$ and $Z$ terms.\n"
"Measuring arbitrary Pauli terms therefore requires a change of basis to diagonalize them. To do this, perform the following transformations,\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang isang measurement ay nagpro-project ng estado ng qubit sa computational basis na $\\{|0\\rangle, |1\\rangle\\}$. Ibig sabihin nito, maaari ka lang sumukat ng mga observable na diagonal sa basis na ito, tulad ng mga Pauli na binubuo lamang ng $I$ at $Z$ na mga term.\n"
"Kaya nangangailangan ng pagbabago ng basis ang pagsukat ng mga arbitrary na Pauli term para i-diagonalize ang mga ito. Para gawin ito, isagawa ang mga sumusunod na transformasyon,\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"    X &\\rightarrow Z = H X H \\\\\n"
"    Y &\\rightarrow Z = H S^\\dagger Y S H,\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"    X &\\rightarrow Z = H X H \\\\\n"
"    Y &\\rightarrow Z = H S^\\dagger Y S H,\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $H$ is the Hadamard gate and $S = \\sqrt{Z}$ is sometimes referred to as the phase gate.\n"
"If you are using an [Estimator](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/1.4/qiskit.primitives.Estimator) to compute expectation values, the basis transformations are automatically performed.\n"
msgstr ""
"kung saan ang $H$ ay ang Hadamard gate at ang $S = \\sqrt{Z}$ ay tinatawag minsan na phase gate.\n"
"Kung gumagamit ka ng [Estimator](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/1.4/qiskit.primitives.Estimator) para sa pagkalkula ng mga expectation value, ang mga basis transformation ay awtomatikong isinasagawa.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Below is an example demonstrating how to prepare a quantum circuit and manually measure the qubit 0 in the X basis,\n"
"qubit 1 in the Y basis and qubit 2 in the Z basis.\n"
"We apply the transformations shown in the previous equation and obtain the following circuit:\n"
msgstr ""
"Nasa ibaba ang isang halimbawa na nagpapakita kung paano maghanda ng quantum circuit at mano-manong sukatin ang qubit 0 sa X basis,\n"
"ang qubit 1 sa Y basis, at ang qubit 2 sa Z basis.\n"
"Inilalapat natin ang mga transformasyong ipinakita sa nakaraang equation at nakukuha natin ang sumusunod na circuit:\n"

